%% The Topic 3 revision sheet — included by KB Part Five and by the RN volume. Must make sense on its own.

## 1. The waves in general

- **Women's movement** — collective, organised action over time to improve women's rights and status.
- **Wave** — a period of intense feminist activity with shared demands. Metaphor made popular by **Martha Weinman Lear**, "The Second Feminist Wave" (*New York Times Magazine*, **1968**); the earlier suffrage campaign then became the "first wave".
- **The four waves** — **first** (c. 1848–1920: vote, property, education, legal personhood); **second** (1960s–80s: work, family, body, sexuality; "the personal is political"); **third** (1990s–2000s: diversity, identity, intersectionality, culture); **fourth** (c. 2012–: digital activism against harassment and violence).
- **Critics of the metaphor** — it follows **white, Western, middle-class** history; hides activism **between** waves; makes generations seem to reject each other; ignores the different timing of the global South. Use it as a **map, not a law**.

## 2. The first wave (c. 1848–1920)

- **What women lacked** — the vote; **coverture** (a married woman's legal identity "covered" by her husband's: no property, contracts or own wages); universities and professions closed; fathers got custody of children.
- **Roots** — Enlightenment "rights of man"; **Olympe de Gouges** (1791), **Wollstonecraft** (1792); training in the **anti-slavery movement**.
- **USA** — **Stanton and Mott** refused seats at the **World Anti-Slavery Convention**, London (**1840**) → **Seneca Falls Convention** (**19–20 July 1848**, New York; about 300 attended, about 100 signed, including **Frederick Douglass**) → **Declaration of Sentiments**: "all men and women are created equal"; demand for the vote. **Anthony** and Stanton's **NWSA** (1869); merged with AWSA in 1890; **Sojourner Truth** (1851); Wyoming vote (1869); **Alice Paul** pickets (1917); **19th Amendment** (**1920**).
- **Britain** — **suffragists**: **NUWSS** (1897), **Millicent Fawcett**, peaceful methods (petitions, lobbying). **Suffragettes**: **WSPU** (1903), **Emmeline and Christabel Pankhurst**, "**Deeds, not words**" — window-smashing, arson, hunger strikes; **Cat and Mouse Act** (1913: hunger strikers released when weak, re-arrested later); **Emily Wilding Davison** died at the Derby (1913). **1918** Act: women over 30 with property; **1928**: equal votes at 21. War work in 1914–18 helped.
- **Other campaigns** — **Caroline Norton** → Custody of Infants Act (1839); Matrimonial Causes Act (1857, divorce); **Married Women's Property Acts** (1870, 1882); **Elizabeth Blackwell** first US woman medical graduate (1849); **Girton College** (1869); **Josephine Butler** against the Contagious Diseases Acts (repealed 1886); temperance.
- **Around the world** — **New Zealand 1893** (Kate Sheppard, first self-governing country); Australia 1902; **Finland 1906** (first in Europe; could also stand); Turkey 1934; France 1944; India and Pakistan 1947 (with independence). Muslim world: **Huda Shaarawi**, Egyptian Feminist Union (**1923**).
- **Principles, goals, strategies** — women are rational citizens (liberal); vote, property, education, custody; conventions, petitions, lobbying, newspapers, militancy.
- **Limits** — mostly white and middle-class (Black Southern women barred until the Voting Rights Act 1965); legal, not social, equality; Western timing.
- **Pakistan** — vote came with independence; first-wave goals (education, property, legal status) shaped APWA, the **1948** Punjab inheritance Act and the **MFLO 1961**.

## 3. The second wave (1960s–1980s)

- **Why** — vote won but inequality in pay, jobs, family, body; the "feminine mystique"; causes: wartime work, more women at university, the **pill** (US 1960), the civil-rights movement, **Beauvoir** (1949).
- **Two streams** — **liberal/equal rights**: **Friedan**, *The Feminine Mystique* (**1963**, "the problem that has no name"); **NOW** (**1966**); lobbying, lawsuits. **Women's liberation/radical**: New York Radical Women, Redstockings; **Millett, Firestone, Hanisch** (1970); consciousness-raising (small groups turning private problems into political awareness), direct action.
- **Key events** — Kennedy's Commission on the Status of Women (1961); US **Equal Pay Act** (1963); **Title VII** (1964); UK Abortion Act (1967); Miss America protest (1968, "freedom trash can"); **Ford Dagenham** strike (1968) → UK **Equal Pay Act 1970**; **Ruskin College** conference (1970; four demands: equal pay, equal education and opportunity, 24-hour nurseries, free contraception and abortion on demand); Chiswick shelter (**1971**); ***Ms.*** (Steinem, 1972); ERA passed Congress (1972, never ratified — **Phyllis Schlafly**); **Title IX** (1972); ***Roe v. Wade*** (**22 January 1973**; overturned by ***Dobbs*** **2022**); UK Sex Discrimination Act (1975); Manifesto of the 343 (France 1971); Iceland women's day off (24 Oct 1975, ~90%); **CEDAW** (1979).
- **"The personal is political"** (Carol Hanisch, 1970) — private problems (housework, marriage, sex, abortion) are shaped by power; most associated with the **second wave** (MCQ 2026).
- **Voices** — Steinem, **Germaine Greer** (*The Female Eunuch*, 1970), Millett, Firestone, **MacKinnon** (harassment as discrimination, 1979), **Angela Davis** (1981).
- **Achievements** — equal pay and anti-discrimination laws; contraception and abortion rights; shelters, rape crisis centres; harassment law; Women's Studies.
- **Blind spots** — white and middle-class (**bell hooks**, 1981; **Audre Lorde**); heterosexual bias (Friedan's "lavender menace", 1969); Western focus (Mexico City 1975 tensions); backlash (**Faludi**, *Backlash*, 1991).
- **Why it faded** — success; divisions ("sex wars"); Reagan–Thatcher conservatism; ERA deadline missed (1982); institutionalisation.
- **Pakistan** — **WAF** (1981) against the **Hudood Ordinances** (1979): a second-wave struggle over body, sexuality and violence; later harassment (2010) and honour (2016) laws.

## 4. The third wave (1990s–2000s)

- **Spark** — **Anita Hill** hearings (Oct **1991**): she testified that Clarence Thomas harassed her; harsh questioning by an all-male committee.
- **Name** — **Rebecca Walker**, "Becoming the Third Wave", *Ms.* (**January 1992**): "I am not a post-feminism feminist. I am the Third Wave" (MCQ 2025); Third Wave Direct Action Corporation (1992).
- **Theory** — **intersectionality** (Crenshaw 1989; hooks; Combahee 1977); **postmodern/queer** (Butler 1990, gender performative); **postcolonial** (Mohanty 1984).
- **Features** — diversity (many womanhoods); intersectionality; individual choice; sex-positivity and reclaiming words; culture — **Riot Grrrl** (1991, zines = homemade magazines; Bikini Kill); **Naomi Wolf**, *The Beauty Myth* (1990); **Eve Ensler**, *The Vagina Monologues* (1996) → V-Day (1998); *Manifesta* (2000); "girl power"; global voices.
- **Laws and campaigns** — US **Violence Against Women Act** (1994); **Vienna** (1993) "women's rights are human rights"; **DEVAW** (1993); **Beijing** (1995); 16 Days of Activism (from 1991); WLUML (1984), Sisters in Islam (1988), Wadud (1992).
- **Postfeminism** — claim that equality is won and feminism unnecessary; criticised by **Angela McRobbie**, **Faludi**; third wave defined itself against it.
- **Criticisms** — too individual ("**choice feminism**"); consumerism ("girl power" as marketing); fragmentation; caricature of the second wave.
- **Effects** — voice for women of colour, LGBT and Southern women; intersectionality in law and policy; harassment reporting; violence as a human-rights issue.
- **Pakistan** — Beijing (1995; **Benazir Bhutto** spoke), NGO boom, attention to rural and minority women, *khawaja siras* (SC 2009; Act 2018), **Islamic feminism** (Riffat Hassan, Asma Barlas).

## 5. The fourth wave (c. 2012–)

- **Definition** — feminist activism using **social media** to share experience, expose **harassment and violence**, organise quickly and hold the powerful accountable; intersectional. **Kira Cochrane**, *All the Rebel Women* (2013).
- **Campaigns** — **SlutWalk** (Toronto 2011); **Everyday Sexism Project** (Laura Bates 2012); Delhi gang-rape protests (Dec 2012) → India's Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013; **#NiUnaMenos** (Argentina 2015; abortion legal Dec 2020); Women's March (21 Jan 2017); **#MeToo** (**Tarana Burke 2006**; global **October 2017** after the Weinstein reports; Alyssa Milano); Ireland's referendum (25 May 2018); Spain's consent law (2022); Iran's "**Woman, Life, Freedom**" (Sept 2022).
- **Features** — digital tools; harassment and violence; intersectionality; bodily autonomy and consent; accountability ("call-out culture"); global speed.
- **Criticisms** — online abuse and doxxing (publishing private details); "slacktivism"; due-process concerns; **digital divide**; manosphere backlash.
- **Pakistan** — **Aurat March** (from **8 March 2018**); #MeToo (2018); **Digital Rights Foundation** (2012; helpline 2016); **PECA 2016**; outrage after **Qandeel Baloch** (2016), the motorway gang rape (2020), **Noor Mukadam** (2021) → Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021; mobile-internet gender gap **38% → 25%** (GSMA, 2024–25).

## 6. The waves compared (2025)

- **Principles** — first: equal natural rights; second: personal is political, patriarchy; third: diversity, intersectionality, choice; fourth: consent, accountability, intersectionality.
- **Goals** — first: vote, property, education; second: pay, jobs, reproduction, violence; third: inclusion, sexuality, identity, global rights; fourth: end harassment and violence, safe spaces.
- **Strategies** — first: petitions, conventions, militancy; second: lobbying, lawsuits, consciousness-raising; third: culture, zines, networks; fourth: hashtags, testimony, mass marches.
- **Theory** — first liberal; second liberal, radical, socialist; third Black, postmodern, queer, postcolonial; fourth intersectional.
- **Effects on women's lives** — political (vote; about a quarter of parliamentarians worldwide are women); legal (property, contracts, divorce; discrimination unlawful); education and work (women at university and in all professions; pay gaps remain); body (contraception, safe abortion in many countries); safety (violence criminalised; shelters); voice (diverse women; harassment costly).
- **Continuity vs change** — new leaders, issues and methods each time, but one core demand: equal dignity and freedom. Treat the waves as **stages of one movement**.
- **Unfinished** — global gap **69.2%** closed (2026), **120 years** to parity; one in three women faces violence (WHO 2021); *Roe* reversed (2022); Pakistan **143rd of 145**.
- **Pakistan's waves** — first ≈ welfare and reform (1947–70); second ≈ legal resistance (1980s); third ≈ NGOs and diversity (1990s–2000s); fourth ≈ Aurat March and digital (2018–). Phases **overlap**.

## 7. The United Nations and women

- **Foundations** — UN Charter (**1945**, "equal rights of men and women"); **CSW** (**21 June 1946**, meets each March); **UDHR** (**10 December 1948** = **Human Rights Day**, MCQ 2018); Convention on the Political Rights of Women (1952); DEDAW (1967).
- **International Women's Year 1975; UN Decade for Women 1976–85** ("Equality, Development and Peace").
- **Four conferences** — **Mexico City 1975** (first; World Plan of Action; UNIFEM/INSTRAW; North–South tensions; MCQ 2016); **Copenhagen 1980** (mid-decade; CEDAW signing ceremony; employment, health, education); **Nairobi 1985** (Forward-Looking Strategies to 2000; large NGO forum); **Beijing 1995** (**4–15 September**; **189** governments; **Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action**; **12 critical areas**; gender mainstreaming; NGO forum at Huairou ~30,000; "women's rights are human rights"; Benazir Bhutto spoke).
- **12 critical areas** — poverty; education; health; violence; armed conflict; economy; power and decision-making; institutional mechanisms; human rights; media; **environment** (area K → MCQ 2023); the girl child.
- **Reviews** — Beijing +5 (2000), +10, +15, +20, +25 (2020), **+30 (March 2025)**: progress in education and laws; slow change in power and pay; violence; **backlash**.
- **CEDAW** — adopted **18 Dec 1979**; in force **3 Sept 1981**; "**an international bill of rights for women**" (MCQ 2026); Committee of 23 experts; reports every four years; **Optional Protocol 1999** (individual complaints; Pakistan not a party). Key articles: 1 (definition), 2 (change laws and customs), **4 (temporary special measures, e.g., quotas)**, 5 (stereotypes), 7 (political life), 10 (education), 11 (employment), 12 (health), **14 (rural women)**, 16 (marriage and family), 29 (disputes → ICJ).
- **Pakistan and CEDAW** — **acceded 12 March 1996** (accession = joining without prior signature); **general declaration** (subject to the Constitution) and **reservation to Article 29(1)**; periodic reports; NGO **shadow reports**; Committee urges repeal of discriminatory laws, enforcement against violence and child marriage.
- **Wider agenda** — **Vienna 1993**; **DEVAW** (Dec 1993); **Cairo ICPD 1994**; **UNSCR 1325** (31 Oct 2000, Women, Peace and Security); MDGs (2000); **UN Women** (July **2010**, operational 2011; **Michelle Bachelet**; merged four bodies incl. UNIFEM); **SDG 5** (2015); HeForShe (2014); Generation Equality Forum (2021); **DAWN** (1984).
- **Assessment** — achievements: global standards, violence as a human-rights issue, national machineries, NGO platforms. Limits: no enforcement; reservations; implementation gaps; "Western values" charge.
- **What it gave Pakistan** — Women's Division (1979); National Plan of Action (1998); NCSW (2000); National Policy (2002); reserved seats (2002); an international platform for activists.

## 8. Roots in South Asia and the Pakistan Movement

- **Social reform** — **Raja Ram Mohan Roy** → Bengal Sati Regulation (**1829**; MCQ 2026); **Vidyasagar** → Widows' Remarriage Act (1856); **Tarabai Shinde** (1882); Age of Consent Act (1891, 10 → 12); **Child Marriage Restraint Act (Sarda Act) 1929**; **Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act 1939**; Shariat Application Act (1937).
- **Muslim reform** — Nazir Ahmad, *Mirat-ul-Uroos* (1869); **Mumtaz Ali**, *Huquq-un-Niswan* (1898) and *Tahzib-e-Niswan* with **Muhammadi Begum**; Sheikh Abdullah and Wahid Jahan Begum's Aligarh girls' school (1906); Begum Rokeya (1905, 1911); **All-India Muslim Ladies' Conference** (from 1914). Pattern: **reform within tradition**; education first.
- **Pakistan Movement** — Muslim League **Central Women's Sub-Committee** (Patna **1938**); **Fatima Jinnah** (*Madar-e-Millat*); **Jahanara Shahnawaz** (only Muslim woman at the Round Table Conferences; first Constituent Assembly with **Shaista Ikramullah**, 1947); **Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan**; Lady Abdullah Haroon (Sindh); **Salma Tassaduq Hussain**, **Viqar-un-Nisa Noon** (1947 Punjab agitation); **Fatima Sughra** (flag on Punjab Civil Secretariat, Feb 1947). **Quaid, Aligarh, 10 March 1944**: "No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you."
- **Partition** — abductions and violence; women's relief work → Women's Voluntary Service, Women's National Guard → **APWA 1949**.
- **Inheritance 1948** — women legislators and a demonstration outside the Punjab Assembly → **West Punjab Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act 1948** (inheritance incl. agricultural land): rights won by appealing to **Islam against custom**.
- **What roots explain** — reform from within Islam; education first; elite leadership; ties to nationalism; vote came with independence.

## 9. The movement in Pakistan — six phases

- **Phase 1 · Welfare and reform (1947–70)** — **APWA** (**1949**, founder and first president **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan**; schools, colleges, clinics, crafts; non-political welfare); PM **Bogra**'s second marriage (1955) → **Rashid Commission** (report 1956) → **MFLO 1961** (Ayub: registration of marriage; arbitration council permission for second marriage; divorce notice; orphaned grandchildren inherit); 1956 Constitution reserved seats; **Fatima Jinnah** vs Ayub (1965).
- **Phase 2 · Constitutional equality (1971–77)** — **1973 Constitution**: Art. 25 (no sex discrimination), 27 (public service), 32 (women in local government), 34 (full participation), 35 (family); Ra'ana Liaquat Governor of Sindh (1973); IWY committee (1975).
- **Phase 3 · Resistance under Zia (1977–88)** — **Hudood Ordinances** (**Feb 1979**; zina, theft, drink, false accusation; rape victims who could not prove rape risked zina charges); **WAF** (Karachi, **Sept 1981**, after the **Fehmida–Allah Bux** stoning sentence; non-hierarchical, non-party, secular); **12 February 1983** Lahore protest against the Law of Evidence (WAF and Punjab Women Lawyers' Association; baton charge; Habib Jalib; **National Women's Day since 2012**); **Safia Bibi** (1983, visually impaired rape victim convicted of zina; FSC acquitted her); **Qanun-e-Shahadat 1984** (Art. 17: two women witnesses in financial matters); Qisas and Diyat (1990/1997, "forgiveness" loophole); Zari Sarfaraz Commission (1983–85, shelved); Sindhiani Tehreek (1982).
- **APWA vs WAF** — APWA: 1949; Ra'ana Liaquat; welfare, education, skills; service and cooperation with the state; non-political; elite charity. WAF: 1981; resist discriminatory laws; protest, lobbying, legal analysis; secular, non-party, confrontational; professional urban women.
- **Phase 4 · Democracy, Beijing, NGOs (1988–99)** — **Benazir Bhutto** (1988, first woman PM of a Muslim country); First Women Bank (1989); women's police stations and **LHW Programme** (1994); Beijing (1995); **CEDAW (1996)**; NGO boom (Shirkat Gah 1975, Aurat Foundation 1986, Simorgh, ASR); HRCP (1987); **Samia Sarwar** (1999, honour killing).
- **Phase 5 · Reforms and machinery (2000–17)** — **NCSW** (2000); 33% local seats (2001); **60 NA / 17 Senate** reserved seats (2002); National Policy (2002); Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2004 (honour crimes); **Protection of Women Act 2006** (rape to Penal Code); Women's Parliamentary Caucus, Fahmida Mirza Speaker, BISP (2008); harassment (2010); acid, anti-women practices (2011); honour and anti-rape amendments, Punjab PWVA (**2016**, after **Qandeel Baloch**); **Elections Act 2017** (5% tickets; 10% women's turnout). = **state feminism** (rights through the state).
- **Phase 6 · New activism (2018–)** — **Aurat March** (8 March 2018: violence, harassment, workers, *khawaja siras*, **bodily autonomy**); **Women Democratic Front**, **Hum Auratein** (2018); digital activism; **Anti-Rape Act 2021**; Women's Property Rights Act 2020; **ICT Child Marriage Restraint Act 2025** (18); ICT Domestic Violence Act 2025; backlash (petitions, "Haya March", online abuse).
- **Other actors** — rural and working women (Sindhiani Tehreek; **Okara** tenants from 2000; LHW union, SC 2012; Sindh Home-Based Workers Act 2018; fisherfolk women); religious women (Jamaat-e-Islami women's wing; **Al-Huda**, 1994, Farhat Hashmi); artists (Kishwar Naheed, Fahmida Riaz; **Tehrik-e-Niswan** 1979, Sheema Kermani; **Ajoka** 1984, Madeeha Gauhar; **Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy**, *Saving Face* 2012, *A Girl in the River* 2016); lawyers (**Asma Jahangir**, **Hina Jilani**: first all-women law firm 1980, AGHS Legal Aid, HRCP 1987); legislators (caucus 2008).
- **History to quote** — **Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed**, *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (**1987**).

## 10. Western influence, past vs emerging, and the shift to the body

- **Channels** — education; the UN (conferences, CEDAW, SDGs); donors and NGOs; media; diaspora scholars.
- **Wave by wave** — first: education, property (vote came with independence; MFLO argued in Islamic terms); second: personal is political, violence (WAF vs Hudood; focus on law); third: diversity, NGOs, human rights (Beijing, CEDAW, Islamic feminism); fourth: digital, #MeToo, bodily autonomy (Aurat March).
- **Not a copy** — local roots; waves arrived **out of order and together**; adapted to Islam and culture; faced a state using religion to restrict women; postcolonial critique is itself Southern. **Verdict:** West supplied tools and platforms; Pakistani women chose and adapted them.
- **Laws showing influence** — Harassment Act 2010 (CEDAW Art. 11); reserved seats 2002 (CEDAW Art. 4, Beijing area G); NCSW 2000 (Beijing area H; Act 2012); honour law 2016 (global campaigns; framed in Islamic and constitutional terms); ICT child-marriage law 2025 (SDG 5.3; CEDAW Art. 16).
- **Past vs emerging (2024)** — past: elite and professional leaders; APWA, WAF, NGOs; welfare, family law, discriminatory laws; lobbying, courts, press; language of Constitution and Islam; cooperation or legal resistance. Emerging: young urban women, students, workers, trans activists; Aurat March collectives, WDF; body, violence, harassment, care, online safety; marches, art, social media; language of consent and intersectionality; independent, confrontational. **Effects:** visibility; inclusion; faster state response (2020 case → 2021 law); backlash and polarisation; class limits (digital divide).
- **Legal rights → bodily autonomy (2026)** — **locus of resistance** = where a movement concentrates its struggle. 1980s WAF: fight laws via courts and lawyers, language of citizenship and Islam. 2020s Aurat March: claim body, safety and choice via streets and social media, language of consent. **Why:** many legal battles won but violence continues; new generation; fourth-wave influence; the body as the site of control (honour, forced marriage, harassment). **Continuity:** the march still demands laws; WAF already defended bodies. Formula: **from rights on paper to rights over one's own body**.
- **Why bodily autonomy provokes** — body linked to family **izzat** (honour); critics hear rejection of family and religion; supporters cite Islam's ban on forced marriage and Article 14 (dignity); wide agreement on substance.

## 11. NGO-isation (2026)

- **NGO-isation** — movement energy moves into **professional, registered, donor-funded NGOs**; activists become staff; campaigns become **projects**; accountability **upwards to donors**. Scholars: **Sonia Alvarez** (1999), **Islah Jad** (2004).
- **Depoliticisation** — turning a struggle about **power** into a **technical** problem managed by experts ("technocratic project" vs "social revolution").
- **WAD** — 1970s Marxist/dependency critique of **WID** (integrate women into development): women were always part of development, but on **unequal, exploitative terms**; adding women to projects leaves structures intact. **GAD** — unequal gender power relations; empowerment. (Topic 4.)
- **How it happened** — military rule closed political space; post-Beijing donor funds; jobs and protection; state liked service delivery. Timeline: Shirkat Gah 1975; WAF 1981 (unfunded); ASR 1983; Simorgh, Aurat Foundation 1985–86; 1990s NGO boom; GRAPs 2000s; INGO regulation from 2015; Aurat March 2018 (self-funded).
- **The case for depoliticisation** — donor-driven agendas; projectisation; professionalisation; upward accountability; urban/class bias; "Western agenda" label; competition and fragmentation; WAD: micro-credit and crafts leave land, feudal power, wages and unpaid care untouched.
- **The case against** — NGOs won laws (2006–2016); kept space open under military rule; services save lives; not all NGOs alike (WAF stayed unfunded); movements revived (Aurat March); growing local funding.
- **Gender mainstreaming** — Beijing 1995; in Pakistan: NPA 1998, National Policy 2002, GRAPs, gender budgeting, NCSW Act 2012, provincial departments after the **18th Amendment (2010)**; critics: checklist exercise, fades with donor money.
- **Verdict** — **partly** depoliticised; but produced laws, data and services; movement re-politicised (WAF legacy, rural struggles, Aurat March). Way forward: **division of labour** — NGOs for expertise and services, movements for mobilisation; local funding; accountability to women.

## 12. Evaluation (2016) and debates

- **Achievements** — family law (MFLO 1961; *khula* — Khurshid Bibi case 1967); criminal law (2006; 2010; 2011; 2016; 2021; DV laws 2013–2025); political voice (60 seats; 33% local; Elections Act 2017; first woman PM 1988, Speaker 2008, CM 2024); institutions (Women's Division 1979; NCSW 2000; commissions; women's police stations; harassment ombudspersons); CEDAW 1996; girls' education accepted.
- **Strengths** — local and Islamic legitimacy; legal expertise (Asma Jahangir, Hina Jilani); resilience under martial law; cross-party caucus; adaptability; research base.
- **Weaknesses** — elite urban leadership; enforcement gap; dependence on state and donors; NGO-isation; "Western agenda" label; religious backlash; divisions (secular/Islamic, liberal/left, old/young).
- **Scorecard** — GGGR 2026 **143/145** (2025: 148/148); LFPR **24.4%**; female literacy ~**53%**; **11** women won general NA seats (2024). Laws changed more than lives.
- **Way forward** — broaden the base (rural, worker, minority, disabled women, *khawaja siras* as leaders); enforce laws; local funding; alliances (parties, unions, ulema, men); local idiom (Constitution, Islam); economic agenda; digital safety; bridge generations.
- **Lessons** — law necessary, not sufficient; gains follow political openings; legitimacy matters; coalitions win; renewal comes from below.
- **Debates** — Aurat March (substance vs slogans; Arts 16–17 right to assemble); "Western agenda"; law vs body; digital reach; NGO capture; **Beijing+30** and global backlash (Roe 2022; Afghanistan since 2021); secular vs Islamic feminism (**Afiya Zia**, 2018); men as gatekeepers or allies.
